-- title:
--   A half-closed cube is contained in the closed cube of the same centre and side
-- statement:
--   For $h \in \mathbb{R}$ and $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$ let
--   $$\widetilde{A}_h(u) \;=\; \prod_{i=1}^{d}\bigl[u_i - \tfrac h2,\ u_i + \tfrac h2\bigr)$$
--   be the half-closed cube of centre $u$ and side $h$ (the cubes that tile $\mathbb{R}^d$), and let
--   $$\overline{A}_h(u) \;=\; \bigl\{x \in \mathbb{R}^d : \|x-u\|_\infty \le \tfrac h2\bigr\},
--   \qquad \|x\|_\infty = \sup_{i} |x_i|,$$
--   be the closed cube with the same centre and side. If $h \ge 0$ then
--   $$\widetilde{A}_h(u) \;\subseteq\; \overline{A}_h(u).$$
--
--   The two cube shapes are used in different parts of the theory — the half-closed ones because they tile the space exactly, the closed ones because they are compact — and this inclusion is the elementary compatibility that lets an estimate proved for the closed cube be applied to the tile.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The hypothesis $h \ge 0$ is what makes the statement hold in the degenerate case $d = 0$, where $\|x\|_\infty$ is the supremum of the empty family and equals $0$, so that membership of the closed cube reduces to $0 \le h/2$ while the half-closed cube is the whole (one-point) space.
